WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.570
On this flow bench, one meter counts water arriving.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:05.850
A second meter counts water escaping.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.710
A pen records how much water remains.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:12.040
The difference between the two rates tells us how

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:14.070
quickly the pen rises or falls.

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:18.190
Seven litres enter each minute and two litres leave.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:21.430
Pair two outgoing marks with two incoming marks.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.770
Five litres stay each minute.

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:27.570
Starting empty, how much collects in three minutes?

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:30.550
Three groups of five give fifteen litres.

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:34.640
Now nine litres enter each minute, but the leak

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:35.650
meter is covered.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:39.140
The pen reads ten litres at minute one and thirty four

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:40.810
litres at minute five.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:42.650
Both flow rates are steady.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:44.390
How fast is water escaping?

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:52.230
From ten to thirty four is a rise of twenty four litres.

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:54.720
From minute one to minute five is four minutes.

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:57.020
Six litres stay each minute.

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:00.180
Nine enter, so three litres per minute escape.

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:03.350
Six litres per minute for four minutes accounts for

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:04.060
the whole rise.

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:07.440
Start a ten minute test with eight litres.

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:10.580
The inlet delivers seven litres per minute throughout.

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:13.830
The leak starts open, losing two litres each

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:15.220
minute, then closes.

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:18.280
The final reading is sixty six litres.

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:20.300
At which minute did the leak close?

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:26.290
Imagine the leak were closed from the start.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:28.520
Eight litres plus seventy would give

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:30.160
seventy eight litres.

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:32.330
The actual finish is twelve litres lower.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:35.860
Each open minute accounts for two missing litres.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:38.730
Twelve divided by two gives six open minutes.

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:41.530
Check the bend at minute six.

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:44.900
While the leak is open, six minutes at five litres per

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:46.920
minute add thirty litres.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:48.190
The reading is now thirty eight.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:51.920
In the last four minutes, seven litres per minute add

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:52.810
twenty eight more.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:55.430
Thirty eight plus twenty eight gives sixty six.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.320
One trap is to keep subtracting the leak after

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:00.170
the valve shuts.

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:03.200
Mark each valve change and find the net rate

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:04.190
for each interval.

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:07.940
If the inlet and outlet rates are equal, the trace is

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:10.190
flat, even while both meters turn.
